1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Secrets are where a single mistake becomes a real breach.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
One leaked API key and an attacker can call your services,

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
spend your budget, and reach your data,

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
all wearing your identity.

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
This lesson covers where secrets should live

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
and how to keep them out of where they could leak.

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Let's get three terms straight

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
because they carry the whole lesson.

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,000
A secret is any credential your app authenticates with,

10
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
an API key, a token, a password.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Leak one and someone else can act as you.

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
An API key specifically is a long-lived string

13
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
that proves who you are to a service

14
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
and it carries whatever access its scope allows.

15
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
So a broad key is a big liability.

16
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Rotation is the habit of replacing a key,

17
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:49,000
either on a regular schedule

18
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
or immediately after you suspect exposure.

19
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
When you rotate, the old credential stops working,

20
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
which means a leaked key has a short shelf life

21
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
instead of an open-ended one.

22
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Keep those three in mind and the rest follows.

23
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Here's the first rule and it's the one people break most.

24
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
A secret never lives anywhere people can read it or share it.

25
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
That means you don't hard-code a key into your source code

26
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,000
and you certainly don't commit it

27
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,000
because once it's in the git history,

28
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
it's effectively public to anyone with the repo.

29
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
It also means keys stay out of your prompts

30
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
and out of clod.md files, which get shared and checked in.

31
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,000
So where do they go?

32
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
You load them from environment variables

33
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
or a dedicated secret store at runtime.

34
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
The mental model to hold onto is this,

35
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,000
where a secret lives and where it's allowed to appear

36
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,000
are two separate questions.

37
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
And the answer to the second one is almost nowhere.

38
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
So where does clod code actually keep your credentials?

39
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
It leans on the operating system,

40
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:54,000
which is the right instinct

41
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
because the OS already has hardened storage

42
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,000
built for exactly this.

43
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
On macOS, your credentials go

44
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
into the encrypted system key chain,

45
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,000
protected by the OS itself.

46
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
On Linux, they land in a credentials file

47
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
locked down to file mode 0600,

48
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
which means only your own user account can read it.

49
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
On Windows, the credentials file sits in your user profile,

50
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
restricted to your account by default.

51
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Notice the pattern across all three.

52
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,000
The store is protected by the operating system

53
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,000
and it isn't a file you commit to a repository.

54
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
It's local, locked, and scoped to you,

55
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,000
which is exactly what you want.

56
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Let's talk about why a key in a prompt is such a trap

57
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
because it feels harmless and it isn't.

58
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
The moment you paste a secret into a prompt,

59
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
you've lost control of it in a few different ways.

60
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
First, the model can echo it right back in its output,

61
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
maybe when summarizing the conversation,

62
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
and now it's in a response you might store.

63
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Second, that prompt gets saved.

64
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
Stored transcripts and request logs

65
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
capture the full text, secret included.

66
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Third, anyone who later has access to those logs

67
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
can simply read your credential,

68
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
so the rule is to exclude secrets

69
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
from context and from logs entirely.

70
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
A secret has no business anywhere near a prompt

71
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
because a prompt is designed to be processed,

72
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
stored, and often reviewed later.

73
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Let me draw the clean line between where secrets live

74
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,000
and where they never appear

75
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
because holding both halves in your head

76
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
is the whole discipline.

77
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Where they live is a short list,

78
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
environment variables, the operating system credential store,

79
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
or a managed secret manager.

80
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
In all of those, access is scoped,

81
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,000
it's logged, and you can revoke it.

82
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Where they never appear is a longer list,

83
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:46,000
and it's everything readable or shareable.

84
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Source code, prompts, clod.md files,

85
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
transcripts, logs, and yes, screenshots,

86
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
which sneak keys into chat threads all the time.

87
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
If a location can be read by a person

88
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
or copied into a message, a secret doesn't go there.

89
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Live in the locked store, appear nowhere casual.

90
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Now, some secrets rotate often

91
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
and you don't want to redeploy every time they do.

92
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Clod code handles that with a setting

93
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
called API Key Helper.

94
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
It's a shell script you point the tool at,

95
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
and its job is to return a key when one is needed.

96
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
The reason this matters is that it lets you fetch

97
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
short-lived tokens from a vault on demand

98
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
instead of pinning a long-lived key in place.

99
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
The helper refreshes on a timer

100
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
or after an authentication failure,

101
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
so when a token expires,

102
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
the next call quietly pulls a fresh one.

103
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
The payoff is that rotation happens at the source.

104
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:44,000
You change the secret in the vault

105
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
and your code never has to know.

106
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
That's the recommended pattern for anything that rotates.

107
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Two habits do most of the work of limiting damage,

108
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:55,000
and you want both.

109
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
The first is least privilege.

110
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Scope every key to only what its job actually needs

111
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,000
and remove everything else.

112
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
A key that reads data should be a read-only key,

113
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
not one that can also delete or administer.

114
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
When a credential can't do much,

115
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
a leak of it can't do much either.

116
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,000
The second habit is rotation.

117
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Replace keys on a regular schedule

118
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
so an old, forgotten key isn't sitting around,

119
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
and rotate immediately the moment you suspect exposure.

120
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Put those together and think about

121
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
what an attacker actually gets.

122
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,000
A narrowly scoped key that was rotated last week

123
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
is a small, short-lived prize.

124
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:33,000
That's the goal.

125
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Make every stolen key nearly worthless.

126
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
Let's close with the everyday hygiene

127
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
that keeps keys from leaking in the first place,

128
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
because most leaks are boring accidents,

129
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
not clever attacks.

130
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Add your env file to gitignore

131
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:53,000
so that local secrets file can never be committed by Reflex.

132
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Run secret scanning against your commits

133
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
so a key that does slip through gets flagged early

134
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
instead of living in history for months.

135
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Watch what you share.

136
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
A key pasted into a shared log, a screenshot,

137
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
or a quick chat message is just as leaked as one in code.

138
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
And read secrets from the store at runtime

139
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
rather than typing them into a prompt.

140
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,000
None of this is hard.

141
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,000
It's a handful of habits that quietly prevent

142
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
the most common exposures.

143
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Secrets belong in a protected store,

144
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
never in your code or prompts.

145
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Clawed code keeps keys in the OS credential store.

146
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
A key in a prompt will leak through output and logs.

147
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
API Key Helper fetches rotating keys on demand,

148
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
and you should scope to least privilege

149
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
and rotate regularly.

150
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
That covers managing secrets and API keys.

151
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Take a breath here, and I'll see you in the next one.

